{"name":"Welcoming Wasp","hex":"#eeaa00","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"welcoming","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["hospitable, accessible and cordial."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of welcome"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/welcoming"},{"term":"wasp","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of many types of stinging flying insect resembling a hornet.","Any of the members of suborder Apocrita, excepting the ants (family Formicidae) and bees (clade Anthophila).\n Any of the members of the family Vespidae."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To move like a wasp; to buzz."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wasp"}],"entries":[{"title":"Wasp","blocks":[{"text":"A wasp is any insect of the narrow-waisted suborder Apocrita of the order Hymenoptera which is neither a bee nor an ant; this excludes the broad-waisted sawflies (Symphyta), which look somewhat like wasps, but are in a separate suborder. The wasps do not constitute a clade, a complete natural group with a single ancestor, as bees and ants are deeply nested within the wasps, having evolved from wasp ancestors. Wasps that are members of the clade Aculeata can sting their prey."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/Vespula_germanica_Richard_Bartz.jpg/330px-Vespula_germanica_Richard_Bartz.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wasp","wikidata":"Q9458574"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Neotype_male_of_Electrostephanus_petiolatus_Brues_in_Baltic_amber_%28AMNH_B-JWJ-260%29.png/500px-Neotype_male_of_Electrostephanus_petiolatus_Brues_in_Baltic_amber_%28AMNH_B-JWJ-260%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Neotype_male_of_Electrostephanus_petiolatus_Brue